module mara.os

// What the operating system does, the same on every platform: the only place
// in the standard library with platform code. Everything over it is portable.
//
// One file for each topic. Each has the same functions in an `#if` block for
// each platform, its foreign calls with them. A C function is bound once per
// platform, by the first file to need it, and all the module's files see it.

// Windows:
// Win32 platform layer — file I/O, virtual memory, dynamic library loading.
// All foreign calls go directly to kernel32.dll (statically linked at build
// time); no C glue file needed.
//
// Type notes:
//   Win32 HANDLE is a pointer-sized opaque token. The kernel uses it as
//   an index into a per-process handle table; user mode never derefs it.
//   We expose file handles as `File_Id :: distinct i64` and bind the
//   relevant kernel32 entry points with i64 in the HANDLE slots — same
//   bits at the ABI level (both pointer and i64 return in RAX on x64),
//   no ptr↔int conversion needed in user-facing code. INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE
//   ((HANDLE)-1 in C) becomes plain `-1` here.
//
//   Module handles (HMODULE from LoadLibraryA) and function-pointer
//   results from GetProcAddress stay as `^byte` for now — they're truly
//   pointer-typed and the call sites cast them at use. Could become
//   Module_Id / Symbol_Addr distinct types later if it'd help.
// Static-linked: clang resolves the symbols against kernel32.lib at link
// time. No dynamic_lib needed — kernel32.dll is implicitly loaded into every
// Win32 process at startup. BOOL is i32 (Win32 zero/non-zero), not Mara bool.

// Linux:
// Linux platform layer — file I/O via libc syscalls. Mirrors the
// Windows API surface (file_open_read/write, file_close, file_size,
// file_read, file_write, file_delete) so user code is identical
// across targets.
//
// Modern glibc has dlopen/dlsym/dlclose in libc.so; older systems
// and musl require -ldl. Add a "dl" foreign block if a target
// system needs that split.
// Static-linked: clang resolves against libc at link time. ssize_t is i64
// on 64-bit Linux; size_t is usize. mode_t is u32. fd is int = i32.
// File_Id is distinct i32, lowers to i32 in IR, matching libc's int — so
// the foreign declarations bind File_Id directly in the fd slots and the
// wrappers don't need any conversion.
